  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe core idea is to employ a hybrid stochastic-local optimization framework that combines exploratory random sampling with gradient-based refinement. This strategy addresses the reliability concerns of previous methods by leveraging a two-stage process: first, generating diverse candidate solutions through randomized perturbations of the parent vector, then applying precise local optimization to exploit the best candidates. The key innovation is the use of an adaptive perturbation schedule that balances exploration and exploitation, while maintaining the integral constraint through dynamic normalization. This approach avoids local minima by systematically sampling the search space while allowing subsequent refinement to zero in on optimal configurations.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe represent h as a piecewise constant function with n_points samples over [0, 2], satisfying:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] * dx = 1 (equivalently \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2)\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx\nThis is equivalent to minimizing:\nC\u2085 = max_lag (np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n\nConstraints:\n- h \u2208 [0, 1]\u207f_points\n- \u2211h = n_points / 2\n\nThe algorithm uses a double-loop process: \n- Outer loop performs random perturbation of the current best vector\n- Inner loop applies gradient-based refinement using L-BFGS-B on the best candidate\n\nThe objective function for minimization is the max_lag value, with gradient approximated via finite differences. The equality constraint \u2211h = n_points / 2 is enforced through a log-barrier penalty in the optimization.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation and initialization\n- Verify initial_h_values has \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 (tolerance 1e-6)\n- If none, create a randomized initial guess:\n   1. Generate random indices to place 1s\n   2. Ensure exactly (n_points//2) 1s and 0s (for even n_points)\n   3. Apply a small sinusoidal perturbation to break symmetry\n\nStage 2: Exploration phase (200 iterations)\n- For each iteration:\n   1. Generate random perturbation vector with magnitude scaling linearly with iteration number\n   2. Add perturbation to current best vector (clamped to [0,1])\n   3. Normalize the vector to maintain \u2211h = n_points / 2\n   4. Evaluate C\u2085 using the exact correlation function\n   5. Update best_c5 and best_h if improvement is found\n\nStage 3: Refinement phase (500 iterations)\n- Use best_h from exploration as initial guess\n- Apply L-BFGS-B optimization:\n   1. Objective: -C\u2085 (to minimize negative correlation)\n   2. Bounds: [0,1] for all elements\n   3. Constraint: \u2211h = n_points / 2 (via equality constraint)\n   4. Track best solution during optimization\n   5. After each iteration, add small random noise to avoid premature convergence\n\nStage 4: Final evaluation\n- Re-evaluate the best solution using exact correlation\n- Compare with fallback parent (scaled to n_points if not provided)\n- Return the solution with lowest C\u2085\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 500 (balances resolution and computation time)\n- Perturbation amplitude: 0.1 * (1 - iteration/200)\n- Initial random seed = 42\n- Perturbation frequency: 10 Hz\n\nVariations:\n- If parent exists: apply 5% sinusoidal perturbation\n- For n_points < 200: interleave with higher frequency pattern\n- If convergence stalls: switch to differential evolution for exploration\n- Use adaptive penalty coefficient for constraint enforcement\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- 200s for exploration phase\n- 300s for refinement phase\n- 100s for final evaluation\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Clamp all values to [0, 1] after each update\n- If \u2211h deviates by >1e-5, apply a rescaling factor\n- Track top 5 candidates and re-evaluate them if optimization stalls\n- Use early stopping if C\u2085 improves by <1e-5 over 10 iterations\n- For failed optimizations, fallback to parent vector scaled to current n_points\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0, 1] within 1e-6\n- Confirm \u2211h == n_points / 2 within 1e-6\n- Ensure C\u2085 is a finite positive number\n\nFinal validation:\n- Re-evaluate the candidate using the exact correlation function\n- Cross-check with a fresh minimize call\n- Ensure the return shape is exactly (n_points,) for h_values\n\nSanity checks:\n- Confirm no NaNs or infinities in the result\n- Validate that the correlation calculation matches the evaluator\n- Ensure the return shape is exactly (n_points,) for h_values",
